{
  "dom_id": "step-q266086",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/9c065ee38659ab3c3dc5b8b2997aa773b560bcf8971f5604421cf0bbe3eab73e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $\\theta $ measured in the usual way. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $0.91,$ calculate the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ to the nearest integer.\n\n${\\theta }_{R}≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$",
  "worked_solution": "The reference angle is the angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we create a right-triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/6f87f2f8bd98ca123c4bc524b6f0e85dada9064b9bbb2c360904c6217a36fb61.png)\n\nSo, we have the following triangle, where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle of $\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/c024d98c0a2d2be7f4c198ffc108e6fbfc5707047608733f07fea72602f04294.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle. \n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{0.91}{1}=0.91.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.91)≈{66}^{∘}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
